        ("What is Object.fromEntries() in ES2019?", "Object.fromEntries() transforms an iterable of [key, value] pairs into an object (the inverse of Object.entries()).", "Easy", "Practical", "const obj = Object.fromEntries([['name', 'A'], ['age', 25]]);", "How is it used with Map?"),
        ("How do you convert a Map to an Object using Object.fromEntries?", "Object.fromEntries(myMap);", "Easy", "Practical", "const map = new Map([['a', 1]]);\nconst obj = Object.fromEntries(map); // { a: 1 }", "How do you convert Object back to Map?"),
        ("What does String.prototype.trimStart() and trimEnd() do in ES2019?", "trimStart() removes whitespace from the start of a string; trimEnd() removes whitespace from the end.", "Easy", "Practical", "'  hi  '.trimStart(); // 'hi  '", "What were their previous non-standard names?"),
        ("What does Array.prototype.flat() do in ES2019?", "flat(depth) creates a new array with sub-array elements concatenated recursively up to the specified depth.", "Easy", "Practical", "[1, [2, [3]]].flat(2); // [1, 2, 3]", "What is the default depth?"),
